7] [17592186413944 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413944 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413944 #edb/ref 1077 "field-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413944 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413944 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f2fdff5c-fcbf-5831-9983-a82bbba778f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26d461a4-4d45-54f1-9397-8e13a8aa54ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413945 #edb/ref 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n(1+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{1+1}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(k+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+1}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((k+1)+2)^{2} & \\le {3}^{(k+1)+1} \\\\ (k+3)^{2} & \\le {3}^{k+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives:\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+3)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+2}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f75ec7c6-91c3-5a79-95d1-5c3dc1cb4b13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n\\ge 2,$ the base case is $P(2).$ So, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n{3}^{2}>1+{2}^{2}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{k}>1+{2}^{k}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{3}^{k+1}>1+{2}^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
